In this lesson we'll look at the distributive law, which is really just a bridge between multiplication and addition. The big idea is simple. If a number is sitting outside a bracket, it gets multiplied by every term inside. That's called expanding, or multiplying out. So three times the quantity x plus four becomes three x plus twelve. And five times the quantity two y minus seven becomes ten y minus thirty five. Easy.

Now the flip side is factoring. Sometimes instead of spreading things out, you want to pull something common back in. For example, six x plus nine has a common factor of three, so you can write it as three times the quantity two x plus three. Same idea with four a plus ten b, which becomes two times the quantity two a plus five b. One thing to watch out for though. Only multiplication distributes over addition. Adding a bracket times something is not the same as multiplying two brackets together, so don't mix those up.

You'll also need to translate everyday English into algebra. Phrases like "five more than x" just mean x plus five. "Three less than y" is y minus three. "Twice n" is two n. "Half of m" is m over two. And "the product of p and q" is simply p q. Now let's see it in action.